WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.970
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:08.650
Today we are going to generate robots with
grammars.

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:11.250
Wait a second.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.250
Grammars?

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:15.690
Of all things, what do grammars have to do
with robots?

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.800
Do we need to teach them grammar to speak
correctly?

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.300
No no, of course not!

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:26.570
To answer these questions, let’s invoke
the Second Law Of Papers, which says that

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:32.680
whatever you are thinking about, there is
already Two Minute Papers episode on that.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:33.860
Even on grammars.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:35.880
Let’s see if it applies here!

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:41.910
In this earlier work, we talked about generating
buildings with grammars.

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:44.330
So how does that work?

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:49.550
Grammars are a set of rules that tell us how
to build up a structure, such as a sentence

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:55.310
properly from small elements, like nouns,
adjectives and so on.

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:59.130
My friend, Martin Ilcik loves to build buildings
from grammars.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:05.019
For instance, a shape grammar for buildings
can describe rules like a wall can contain

00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:12.470
several windows, below a window goes a window
sill, one wall may have at most two doors

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:14.630
attached, and so on.

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:19.870
A later paper also used a similar concept
to generate tangle patterns.

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:25.250
So this grammar thing has some power in assembling
things after all!

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:29.380
So, can we apply this knowledge to build robots!

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:35.620
First, the robots in this new paper are built
up as a collection of these joint types, links

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:40.740
and wheels, which can come in all kinds of
sizes and weights.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:46.390
Now, our question is, how do we assemble them
in a way so that they can traverse a given

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:48.430
terrain effectively?

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:52.030
Well, time for some experiments!

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:53.570
Look at this robot.

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:59.040
It has a lot of character, I must say, and
can deal with this terrain pretty well.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.960
Now look at this poor thing.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:07.299
Someone in the lab at MIT had a super fun
day with this one I am sure.

00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:13.440
Now, these can sort of do the job, but now,
let’s see the power of grammars and search

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:19.320
algorithms in creating more optimized robots
for a variety of terrains!

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:21.550
First, a flat terrain.

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:25.850
Let’s see…yes, now we’re talking!

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:31.090
This one is traversing at great speed… and
this one works too.

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:37.050
I like how it was able to find vastly different
robot structures that both perform well here.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:42.849
Now, let’s look at a little harder level,
with gapped terrains.

00:02:42.849 --> 00:02:46.900
Look, oh wow, loving this.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:52.400
The algorithm recognized that a more rigid
body is required to efficiently step through

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.410
the gaps.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:03:02.820
And now, I wonder what happens if we add some
ridges to the levels, so it cannot only step

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:06.930
through the gaps, but has to climb?

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:13.010
Let’s see…and we get those long long limbs
that can indeed climb through the ridges.

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:14.010
Excellent!

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:19.940
Now, add a staircase, and see who can climb
these well!

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:28.810
The algorithm says, well, someone with long
arms and a somewhat elastic body.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:33.830
Let’s challenge the algorithm some more!

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:37.959
Let’s add, for instance, a frozen lake.

00:03:37.959 --> 00:03:41.220
Who can climb a flat surface that is really
slippery?

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.860
Does the algorithm know?

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:52.910
Look, it says, someone who can utilize a low-friction
surface by dragging itself through it, or

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:55.940
someone with many legs.

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:57.040
Loving this.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:01.510
Now, this is way too much fun, so let’s
do two more.

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:04.629
What about a walled terrain example?

00:04:04.629 --> 00:04:07.480
What kind of robot would work there?

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:14.480
One with a more elastic body, carefully designed
to be able curve sharply, enabling rapid direction

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:15.480
changes.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:21.189
But it cannot be too long, or else it would
bang its head into the wall.

00:04:21.189 --> 00:04:26.250
This is indeed a carefully crafted specimen
for this particular level.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:32.830
Now, of course, real-world situations often
involve multiple kinds of terrains, not just

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:33.830
one.

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:39.479
And of course, the authors of this paper know
that very well, and also asked the algorithm

00:04:39.479 --> 00:04:46.379
to design a specimen that can traverse walled
and ridged terrains really well.

00:04:46.379 --> 00:04:51.440
Make sure to have a look at the paper, which
even shows graphs for robot archetypes that

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.219
work on different terrains.

00:04:53.219 --> 00:05:00.009
It turns out, one can even make claims about
the optimality, which is a strong statement.

00:05:00.009 --> 00:05:02.740
I did not expect that at all.

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:09.080
So, apparently, grammars are amazing at generating
many kinds of complex structures, including

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:10.659
robots.

00:05:10.659 --> 00:05:15.629
And note that this paper also has a followup
work from the same group where they took it

00:05:15.629 --> 00:05:22.110
a step further, and made figure skating and
breakdancing robots.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:23.910
What a time to be alive!

00:05:23.910 --> 00:06:06.270
The link is also available in the video description
for 

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:12.059
that one.

00:06:12.059 --> 00:06:20.819
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

